- feat(examples/103/cs103-stress): new 103 stress/performance harness
  (N device slaves + N masters, spontaneous background, class-1/2 polling,
  command and GI RTT, allocation/GC counters, optional burst mode).
  Measured on this machine (Release, 20 s):
  * 100 devices, steady: 2.6k ASDU/s, class-1 RTT avg 1.46 ms, 0 timeouts,
    268 MiB working set.
  * 100 devices, burst (16 frames per class-1 poll): **71.5k ASDU/s**,
    class-1 RTT avg 2.31 ms, 0 timeouts, 272 MiB.
- fix(CS101): **file transfer no longer deadlocks**. Diagnosed from a clean
  packet-level repro (master pushes a 1025-byte file to the slave); three
  independent defects had to be fixed together — each one alone still stalls:
  * `SecondaryLinkLayerBalanced` only sent a link-layer ACK when the
    *application* returned "handled". Link-layer acknowledgement is not the
    application's decision: if the master has no ASDU handler registered (the
    common case) it stayed silent, and the peer reset the link on ACK timeout.
    It now always answers (single-char ACK or fixed ACK, following
    `UseSingleCharACK`), which is what `SecondaryLinkLayerUnbalanced` already
    did. FCB/FCV validation now only decides whether the payload is delivered to
    the application, not whether the frame is acknowledged — a repeated frame
    must be ACKed so the sender stops repeating.
  * `Iec101Client.RunLoopAsync` never called `_fileClient.HandleFileService()`,
    while the single-step `RunAsync` did. File-transfer segments are not
    triggered by peer traffic, so the master sent exactly one segment and then
    waited forever in `SEND_SECTION`. Both paths now drive the file state
    machine at the top of each iteration.
- fix(CS101): `StreamByteSource` read via the legacy
  `ReadAsync(byte[], int, int, CancellationToken)` overload. On any `Stream`
  that does not override that overload the call falls back to the synchronous
  `Read` path, which never sees the cancellation token — so the per-read timeout
  was silently ignored and the receive loop blocked forever. This affected
  serial transports and any user-supplied `Stream`. Now uses
  `ReadAsync(Memory<byte>, CancellationToken)` uniformly, with the same
  `Math.Max(timeout, 1)` guard as `AsyncByteQueue`.
- fix(CS101): `SerialTransceiverFT12(SerialPort, ...)` read `SerialPort.BaseStream`
  in its constructor, which throws `InvalidOperationException` unless the port is
  already open — making the documented ownership contract ("caller owns the port,
  the library opens it in `StartAsync`") unreachable and crashing every serial
  example at construction time. The stream is now resolved lazily on first I/O.
- fix(CS101): unbalanced master silently dropped user data when the target
  address had no registered slave connection (`SlaveAddress` left at its `0`
  default, or simply not `AddSlave`d). `SendConfirmed` / `SendNoReply` now throw
  `ArgumentException`, consistent with `GetStateOfSlave`, instead of losing the
  message without a trace.
- fix(CS101): `Iec101Server.LinkLayerAddressOtherStation` was dead after startup.
  `RunAsync` created the balanced primary link layer into a **local** variable
  that shadowed the field the property setter writes to, so assigning the peer
  address at runtime had no effect (the property's `!= null` guard never fired).
- test(CS101): closed the two largest end-to-end coverage gaps. All previous
  CS101 end-to-end tests ran BALANCED over TCP with a 1-byte address — the
  server's *default* mode (UNBALANCED) and the serial/`Stream` transports had no
  automated coverage at all. New: `CS101FileTransferTests` (download, upload, and
  the "unconsumed ASDU must still be ACKed" contract), `CS101UnbalancedTests`
  (GI and single-command round trips, including the class-2 polling → ACD →
  class-1 fetch sequence), `CS101SerialAndStreamTests` (master↔slave over an
  in-memory duplex pipe, plus the constructor/ownership contract). Each fix above
  is covered by a test that was verified to fail when the fix is reverted.
  Full suite: **237 passing**.
- fix: **production-readiness hardening** (from a four-dimension deep review:
  concurrency, resource lifetime, protocol robustness, API/ops). Fixed the items that
  could cause silent field failures — each verified with a regression test that fails
  when the fix is reverted:
  * **CS104 shared timer could silently stop all T1/T2/T3** (high). The last
    `Unregister` decremented the live count to zero and called the *static*
    `ForceShutdown()`, which disposed the process-wide timer and cleared every shard
    heap. A connection registering concurrently got its entry wiped (`HeapIndex = -1`,
    never armed) → its T1/T2/T3 never ran, a half-open TCP was never declared dead,
    `ConnectionClosed` never fired, the client never reconnected — and no log line was
    written. Also, an *old* instance reaching zero would shut down whichever instance
    was current. Now: shutdown is instance-scoped behind `SingletonGate`, re-checks the
    count under the lock, only runs when `ReferenceEquals(_instance, this)`, and
    `Register` throws `ObjectDisposedException` instead of returning a never-armed
    entry. `Instance` uses `Volatile.Read/Write` for correct double-checked locking on
    weak memory models.
  * **CS104 disposed `_sendLock` while a send could still hold it** (high). The comment
    assumed no sender can hold the lock, but `CleanupConnection()` is invoked from
    `RequestTransportClose`'s `Task.Run`, `OnTcpClosed`, `Rebuild` and `SafetyDispose`
    on arbitrary threads. `SemaphoreSlim.Dispose()` then made the `finally`'s
    `Release()` throw `ObjectDisposedException`, **masking the original send
    exception**; `WaitAsync` on a disposed semaphore throws ODE instead of OCE, so
    retry logic keyed on `OperationCanceledException` took the wrong branch. The
    semaphore (no unmanaged resources when `WaitHandle` is untouched) is no longer
    disposed.
  * **CS104 signalled STARTDT_CON success on Dispose** (high). `Dispose` used
    `TrySetResult(true)` to wake the handshake waiter, so `StartDataTransferAsync`
    returned *success* when the connection was torn down mid-handshake;
    `Iec104Client` then set `_dataTransferStarted = true` and all later calls silently
    no-opped while STARTDT had never completed. Dispose now cancels the waiter.
  * **CS101 `_fatalError` was never reset** (fatal). A single port-denied / write
    failure made `RunLoopAsync`'s loop condition false forever — the master stopped
    silently, `StartAsync` still returned normally, and there was no event and no
    `Warning` log. `StartAsync` now resets it, and the fatal path logs at `Warning`
    (it was `Debug`, invisible at production log levels).
  * **CS101 `StartAsync` blocked forever on the serial path** (fatal for callers).
    It returned `RunLoopAsync(...)`, so `await StartAsync()` never completed —
    contradicting its own XML doc ("non-blocking") and the semantics of CS103/CS104.
    It now starts an internal loop task and returns immediately.
  * **CS104 `TypeHandlers` was dead configuration** (high). The receive path always
    did `new AsduView(asdu, _al)` and never injected the registry, so user-registered
    private types (e.g. Xuji 166/168) still threw "Unknown ASDU type id". The registry
    is now passed through `ApduEndpoint` into `ApduConnection`.
  * **CS104 `StopAsync()` → `StartAsync()` left the server "fake online"** (high).
    `StopAsync` cancelled every redundancy group's drain loop and `Init` is
    idempotent, so after a restart the server accepted connections and received
    frames but `EnqueueAsync` events were **never sent** — no exception, no log.
    `StartAsync` now rebuilds the groups.
  * **CS101 unbalanced master could silently drop remote-control commands** (high).
    `SendConfirmed` / `SendNoReply` did a check-then-set on `nextMessage` with no lock
    while the loop thread consumed it — two concurrent sends overwrote each other.
    Now guarded by a per-slave `MessageGate`.
  * `IsUserDataClass1QueueFull` / `Class2QueueFull` compared with `==` (missed
    overflow) and read without the lock; now `>=` under the lock.
  * CS104 `AsduReceived` is now exception-isolated (a throwing subscriber used to skip
    the S-frame acknowledgement and bubble into the transport); CS103 already did this.
  * `Iec104Client.SendAsync` throws `InvalidOperationException("连接未建立…")` instead of
    NRE.
  * Guards: `tests-cs104/ProductionHardeningTests.cs` (5 tests). Full suite
    **272 passing**.
- fix(CS101): **reconnect lost the first frame of each new session**. Found by the new
  soak test (12 disconnect/reconnect cycles). Symptom: from the 2nd cycle on, the
  slave-pushed `ACTIVATION_CON` of a general interrogation was silently dropped —
  the data frame and `ACT_TERM` arrived, only the very first frame was missing.
  Root cause: `PrimaryLinkLayerBalanced._nextFcb` (the slave's *sending* frame-count
  bit) was initialised once and never reset. On reconnect the peer resets its
  expectation to FCB=1 (per `RESET_REMOTE_LINK`), but the local `_nextFcb` kept the
  value from the previous session, so the peer saw a duplicate frame and — by design
  in 101, which prefers dropping to re-executing — discarded it.
  Fix: `ResetSequenceState()` on `PrimaryLinkLayerBalanced`, invoked from
  `SecondaryLinkLayerBalanced`'s `RESET_REMOTE_LINK` branch (the exact point where the
  peer resets its own expectation). Verified by reverting it: the reconnect test fails
  immediately with "0 ASDUs in round 1".
- test: **production-readiness test suite** (10 new tests, `RobustnessFuzzTests` +
  `SoakAndReconnectTests`), covering what the existing tests never did:
  * **Fuzz**: 2,000 random 64-byte writes into the FT1.2 framer (no crash, no
    deadlock, bounded buffering), truncated/over-long frames, 500 malformed APCI
    buffers, 3,000 random ASDU byte blobs through the CS101/CS103 decoders —
    asserting only *controlled* exceptions (`ASDUParsingException`) escape, never
    `IndexOutOfRange` / `NullReference` / `ArgumentOutOfRange`.
  * **Soak/reconnect**: 12 CS104 reconnect cycles (asserting the managed heap grows
    < 8 MB and all server sessions are reclaimed), 8 CS101 balanced reconnect cycles,
    8 concurrent CS104 clients soaking for 10 s, and 20 clients that connect-then-drop
    immediately (asserting zero leaked server sessions).
  Full suite: **267 passing**.
- fix(CS103): **follow-up "half-fix" audit (2026-11-08)** — same exercise as CS101/CS104
  for the 103 package; nine places where a fix had covered only one side of a symmetric
  pair are now fixed:
  * **Serial transport read `SerialPort.BaseStream` in its constructor** — that property
    throws `InvalidOperationException` ("The BaseStream is only available when the port
    is open") unless the port is already open, while the class contract is "caller
    passes a closed port, the library opens it". `SerialLinkTransport` now resolves the
    stream lazily in `Open()`/first I/O (`EnsureInner`). This is the *same* defect fixed
    in CS101's `SerialTransceiverFT12` earlier in this release — the whole serial path
    would have failed on real hardware before doing any I/O, and every existing 103 test
    used in-memory pipes so it never showed. A regression test now constructs the
    transport with a closed `SerialPort` and asserts the exact exception is gone
    (verified to fail when reverted).
  * **Slave-side subscriber callbacks had no exception isolation**: `Iec103Server.Raise*`
    were bare `?.Invoke`, so one throwing subscriber killed that session's read loop
    silently (the client had `SafeInvoke` all along). Now all raise paths are isolated,
    `SessionCore.DispatchAsdu` has local try/catch (including a dedicated
    `Iec103UnknownAsduException` handler for the Throw policy), and `RaiseLinkEvent`/
    `RawFrameSent`/`FrameTraced` on the client are isolated too. While doing this, a
    latent infinite-recursion hazard was removed: `SafeInvoke` used to re-enter
    `RaiseLinkEvent` on failure.
  * **`LinkTransaction103` correlation was never wired**: `Begin()` was called with no
    arguments, so `ExpectedAddress`/`AcceptedFunctions` stayed null and `IsMatch`
    only checked `PRM=0` — a stale ACK from the previous round could be consumed by a
    class-1/2 data call. `ExecuteLinkServiceAsync` now takes an accepted-function set
    and the operations pass `ControlFrameResponses` / `DataCallResponses` /
    `SendConfirmResponses`.
  * **`LinkTransaction103.Complete()` masqueraded as a timeout**: completion only
    released the semaphore, and the reader then threw `OperationCanceledException`,
    which matched the "frame timed out → retransmit" branch. Added
    `Iec103TransactionAbortedException` and re-threw it before the timeout branch in
    both wait paths, so ending a transaction no longer triggers a bogus resend.
  * **Passive disconnect left state behind**: `OnTransportClosed` did not cancel the
    lifetime token (in-flight transactions then waited the full
    `MessageTimeoutMs × (MaxRepeat+1)`) and `ConnectAsync` overwrote the old loop/CTS
    without disposing them (two readers could contend on the same stream). Both fixed.
  * **Slave-side gaps**: `UnknownTypePolicy.Throw` was not implemented (only Log
    existed); `Iec103UnknownAsdu.Session` was hard-coded `null`; the slave's frame-trace
    text callback was an empty method body. All three now behave like the client.
  * **`StreamLinkTransport` never marked itself failed** on read/write errors, so
    `IsOpen` stayed true and reads kept retrying a dead stream; TCP-session
    `TransportClosed` was never raised (three transports had three different
    semantics); slave broadcast used sync-over-async
    (`.AsTask().GetAwaiter().GetResult()`) despite the contract forbidding it.
  * **Guards**: `CS103SlaveIsolationTests.cs` (3 tests) proves a throwing
    `AsduReceived` / `UnknownAsduReceived` / `FrameTraced` subscriber on the *slave*
    cannot kill the session, and `CS103SerialTests` gained the closed-`SerialPort`
    construction test.
- fix(CS101): **follow-up "half-fix" audit (2026-11-08)** — a deep re-review of the
  CS101 file-transfer work found several more places where only one of two symmetric
  paths had been repaired. These are now fixed:
  * `FileServer.HandleFileTransmission` gated all timeout handling on
    `_selectedFile != null`, which is only set for **uploads**. The download states
    (`WAITING_FOR_SECTION_READY` / `RECEIVE_SECTION`, driven by `_fileReceiver`)
    were excluded entirely: if the master stopped mid-transfer, the slave never timed
    out and never called `_fileReceiver.Finished(TIMEOUT)`. It now tracks both
    directions, notifies the receiver, and clears checksums/state.
  * `FileServerState.SEND_ABORT` was a **dead state**: four assignment sites but no
    handler, so DEACTIVATE_FILE was never sent (only a 3 s timeout escaped).
    `SendDeactivateAndReset()` now emits it and resets both directions' state,
    symmetric with `FileClient.AbortFileTransfer`.
  * `FileServer` download path never verified the section checksum: `F_SG_NA_1`
    accumulated no checksum and `F_LS_NA_1` unconditionally replied
    `POS_ACK_SECTION`. It now accumulates and compares, replying
    `NEG_ACK_SECTION / CHECKSUM_FAILED` — symmetric with the client.
  * `FileClient.AbortFileTransfer` notified only `_fileReceiver`; the upload-side
    `_fileProvider.TransferComplete(false)` was missing (the timeout path did both).
    Fixed. The `F_FR_NA_1` / `WAITING_FOR_REQUEST_FILE` rejection branch now aborts
    and resets state instead of leaving the state machine stuck (which caused a
    second `TransferComplete(false)` on timeout).
  * `HandleFileService` let a `LinkLayerBusyException` or send failure escape into
    `Iec101Client.RunLoopAsync`'s catch, which **exits the whole receive loop** while
    the file state stayed in `SEND_SECTION`. Busy now retries next round; hard
    failures notify both sides and reset to IDLE.
  * `Iec101Server` created `_linkLayer` lazily inside `RunAsync`, so pre-start calls
    to `DIR`, `SendLinkLayerTestFunction`, `SetReceivedRawMessageHandler` /
    `SetSentRawMessageHandler` threw NRE (the client creates it in its constructor).
    `EnsureLinkLayerEngine()` now runs in all three constructors; primary/secondary
    layers are still assembled in `RunAsync` (link mode can change until then).
  * PortDenied/fatal-error guard was registered only in `StartAsync` (and re-added on
    every call), while `_fatalError` was only checked by single-step `RunAsync` — so
    neither drive path actually stopped a dead-port loop. Registration moved to
    `InitializeLinkLayer`/constructor, `RunLoopAsync` now checks `_fatalError`, and
    `Iec101Server` got the same guard (it had none). `Iec101Server.StartAsync` now
    also disposes the previous CTS like the client does.
  * `IServerApplicationLayer.ResetCUReceived(bool onlyFcb)` ignored its parameter
    and always cleared both queues; RESET_FCB (FC=7, `onlyFcb=true`) must not discard
    pending class-1/2 data. Now honoured.
  * **Guards**: `tests-cs104/CS101FileFailureTests.cs` (3 tests, each verified to
    fail when the corresponding fix is reverted) covers download-direction timeout
    → `Finished(TIMEOUT)` + state reset, `SEND_ABORT` → DEACTIVATE_FILE actually
    emitted, and pre-start `DIR` / raw-handler / link-test access on `Iec101Server`.
- chore(examples): `examples/104/cs104-server-file` → `examples/101/cs101-slave-file`
  and `examples/104/cs104-client-file-upload` → `examples/101/cs101-master-file`.
  Both use `Iec101Server` / `Iec101Client` and were misfiled under 104 with 104
  names; namespaces, `.csproj` names, solution entries and the solution folder
  nesting (104 → 101) were updated. Both examples now run BALANCED (the file
  state machine needs the slave to push frames), the master demonstrates **both**
  directions (upload *and* download), waits for the link to come up instead of
  blindly sleeping, and bounds every wait with a timeout so a stall reports a
  failure instead of hanging.
- feat(examples): new `examples/101/cs101-stress-500` — 101 stress harness with the
  **same measurement口径** as `examples/104/cs104-stress-500` (same device/master
  topology, spontaneous rate, command cadence, GI cadence, per-ASDU point count),
  so 101 and 104 can be compared directly. 500 devices = 500 device servers on
  their own ports + 500 master clients in one process.
- test: **500-device re-measurement of all three protocols** (v6.1.0 working tree,
  Release, single machine — Ryzen 7 5800H, 16 logical cores, .NET 8.0.28):

  | | 101 | 103 | 104 |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | 链路/会话建立 | 500/500 | 500/500 | 500/500 |
  | 合计 ASDU 吞吐 | 11,244 /s | 12,869 /s | 10,719 /s |
  | 命令 RTT P99 | 76.6 ms | — | **1.04 ms** |
  | CPU（整机占比） | 12.8 % | — | 4.8 % |
  | 工作集 | 138 MB | 323 MB | 355 MB |
  | 超时 / 错误 | **0 / 0** | **0 / 0** | 0 解码错误，**但有 7 % 链路失效（见下）** |

  Findings: 101's per-command round trip is ~70x slower than 104's (P99 76 ms vs
  1 ms) because FT1.2 is one-frame-one-ACK with no window — a link-layer property,
  not a defect; conversely 101 uses only 39 % of 104's per-device memory (283 KB vs
  727 KB) since it has no k-buffer/APCI adapter/T1T2T3 timers. Reports:
  `examples/101/cs101-stress-500/STRESS-TEST-REPORT.md`,
  `examples/103/cs103-stress/STRESS-TEST-REPORT.md`,
  `examples/104/cs104-stress-500/STRESS-TEST-REPORT.md` (+ raw logs/JSON under each
  tool's `results/`).
- fix(CS104): **fixed the "receive-only zombie link" defect** found by the 500-device
  stress run. Under load ~7 % of links (36/500) silently stopped accepting sends:
  `ConnectionClosed` never fired, `IsActivated`/`Online` still reported true, yet every
  later `SendAsync` threw "Writing is not allowed after writer was completed".
  Diagnosis (instrumented stack + link-state snapshot) uncovered **two** root causes,
  not one — the initial "rollback" theory was only the surface:
  1. **A register-after-await race was the actual trigger.** `SendAsduAsync` /
     `SendEncodedAsduAsync` took N(S) (advancing `_nextSendSeq`) but registered the
     frame in the k-window ring buffer only *after* `await _sink.SendAsync`. In that
     window `unacked == 0` while `nextSendSeq` had already advanced, so a perfectly
     legitimate redundant acknowledgement `N(R) = nextSendSeq-1` fell outside
     `KWindow.Ack`'s legal interval `[S, nextSendSeq]` and was rejected as a protocol
     error. Every one of the 33 instrumented errors had exactly this signature
     (`NR out of range: NR=4 nextSendSeq=5 unacked=0`). Fix: `TakeSendSeq` +
     `RegisterSent` + APCI header now happen in the **same `_kLock` critical section**,
     both send paths. `CheckSequenceNumbers=false` behaviour is unchanged.
  2. **Failure/close reporting was tied to TouchSocket's `Closed` event.** Protocol
     errors were handled by `CloseAsync("protocol error")` from inside the receive
     callback; that left the socket `Online`, completed the adapter's `PipeWriter`, and
     *never* fired `Closed` — the only place `ConnectionClosed` was dispatched from.
     Fix: `ApduEndpoint.FailConnection(reason)` now owns the path — it synchronously
     sets `IsActive=false`, records `CloseReason`, dispatches `ConnectionClosed` exactly
     once (`ApduConnection.NotifyClosed` is idempotent), and *asynchronously* requests
     transport close, so no socket teardown happens inside a receive callback. It is
     used by protocol errors, fatal timeouts, and send failures alike; both hosts
     (`Iec104Client` / `Iec104Session`) no longer call `CloseAsync` from
     `OnTcpReceived`. `LastProtocolErrorDetail` now records which branch fired
     (NS mismatch / NR out of range / short ASDU) with window state, plus a warning
     log — previously there was no diagnostic at all.
  - `KWindow.RollbackSendSeq` / `SequenceNumbers.Decrement` **removed**: rolling back
    N(S) on a send exception is unsound because a thrown send may still have reached
    the peer (sequence ambiguity is unrecoverable under 104). A failed send with an
    allocated N(S) now fails the connection (`IoError`) instead. A failed connection
    rejects further sends with a diagnostic `OperationCanceledException("connection
    failed: …")` and ignores any frames arriving before transport teardown.
  - `Dispose`/`NotifyClosed` now also clear `IsActive`, so the state query can never
    disagree with `ConnectionClosed`.
  - **The host-side cleanup also no longer depends on `OnTcpClosed`.** The original
    symptom was two-layered: not only was `ConnectionClosed` not dispatched, but
    `CleanupAsync()` (wake pending command-confirmation waiters) / server session
    unregister never ran either whenever TouchSocket's `Closed` callback did not
    fire — callers would hang until `CommandConfirmationTimeout`. `Rebuild` now takes
    an idempotent `onFailed` action that `RequestTransportClose` invokes in a
    `finally` after the async transport close, unconditionally (even if closing the
    transport itself throws). `Iec104Client` wires it to `CancelLife` +
    `CompleteAllCanceled()`, `Iec104Session` to `CancelLife` + `UnregisterSession`.
    This is what makes a *send* failure (not just a protocol error) fully reportable.
  - **Follow-up "half-fix" audit (2026-11-08)**: a full re-review of this same change
    found seven more places where the new failure mechanism had only been applied to one
    of several symmetric paths. All are now fixed:
    * **Structural guard**: `ApduEndpoint.RequestTransportClose` now calls
      `CleanupConnection()` in its own `finally` (releasing the shared timer
      registration / `_liveCount` and the state machine). Previously this was left to
      the host `onFailed` callbacks — i.e. one more "only one side wired" risk.
      `SharedTimeoutScheduler.LiveCount` was added as a diagnostic, and a regression
      test asserts the release happens **even with no host callback at all**
      (verified to fail when the endpoint's own cleanup is removed). Both hosts still
      call `CleanupConnection()` defensively; all paths are idempotent.
    * `SendUAsync` / `SendSAckAsync` wrote to the sink with neither a `_failed` guard
      nor a failure path, while both I-frame paths had been fixed — U/S write
      failures escaped into `OnTcpReceived` (no catch) or were swallowed as
      "transient" by the timer scheduler, leaving a dead link polled forever. All
      three send paths now share `ThrowIfFailed()` and call `FailConnection(IoError)`.
    * `RedundancyGroup.DrainAsync` caught `OperationCanceledException` as "loop
      cancelled" and `break`ed. The new `_failed → OCE("connection failed: …")`
      signal therefore **regressed** standby promotion: a dead primary stopped the
      drain loop permanently and events piled up. It now distinguishes token
      cancellation (break) from connection failure (requeue + promote).
    * `StopDataTransferAsync` never cleared `_dataTransferStarted`, so after STOPDT
      a later `StartDataTransferAsync` silently no-opped (asymmetric with the
      failure path which does reset it). Now reset on STOPDT_CON.
    * `Iec104Server.RaiseConnectionEvent` handled `Activated` / `ConnectionClosed`
      but not `Deactivated`: a STOPDT-deactivated session stayed the redundancy
      group's active target and kept receiving I-frames. Added `OnDeactivated`.
    * `Rebuild`'s `closeAsync` was hardcoded `CloseAsync("timeout")` for *all*
      failure reasons; it now receives the real `ConnectionCloseReason`.
    * Removed the now-orphaned `ApduConnection.MarkCloseReason` (vestige of the old
      "record reason, wait for `OnTcpClosed` to dispatch" mechanism that caused the
      zombie links in the first place).
  - **Verification**: `tests-cs104/CS104ConnectionFailureTests.cs` (12 tests, each
    verified to fail on the pre-fix code) covers send-failure → fail + event,
    event idempotency, NR-out-of-range → fail + event + transport-close request,
    the register-after-await race with a gated sink, post-dispose state,
    post-failure frame suppression, **cleanup still runs when `OnTcpClosed`
    never fires / when transport close throws**, and **U/S frame send failures
    and post-failure rejection on all three send paths**. The legacy
    `KWindow_SendFailure_ReleasesSlot` assertion was a false green (it passed
    because the second send *threw*); it now asserts the real contract.
    Full suite **257/257** (Debug + Release). 500-device stress re-run after the
    fix (three independent runs, all identical): **500/500 links up, 0 disconnects,
    0 decode errors, 0 command timeouts, 0 command failures, 0 GI timeouts,
    ~11,180 ASDU/s** — see `examples/104/cs104-stress-500/results/`.
- chore(examples/104/cs104-stress-500): the tool's `catch (Exception)` lumped all
  command failures into one anonymous counter, which is why the defect above stayed
  hidden. It now attributes failures by exception type + message, keeps the **full
  exception text and stack of the first occurrence**, records the failure
  **concentration across masters** (what tied the 36 failed masters to the 36
  server-side session closes), captures a **link-state snapshot** at the first
  failure (`Online` / `IsActivated` / `LastCloseReason` / TouchSocket `Closed`
  fired?), and prints "still-active masters N/M" in the report.

Changes to version 6.0.2
------------------------
- fix(CS104): `ControlConfirmMatcher.ReadSelectBit` mis-read the Select bit for
  setpoint command confirmations. It always read the byte at `ASDU header + IOA`
  (correct only for C_SC/C_DC/C_RC, whose SCO/DCQ/RCO immediately follows the IOA).
  For C_SE_NA_1 / C_SE_NB_1 the 2-byte value precedes the QOS, and for C_SE_NC_1 a
  4-byte float precedes it — so the receive side read the low byte of the *value*
  instead of the QOS byte. When that byte's bit7 was set (e.g. normalized value
  0.5f -> 0xFF), the ACTIVATION_CON was keyed as select=true while the sender's key
  was select=false, the confirmation never matched, and the command timed out after
  `CommandConfirmationTimeout`. C_BO_TA_1 (no Select bit) was also mis-read.
  Fix: `SelectQualifierOffset(TypeID)` now returns the per-type qualifier offset
  (2 for C_SE_NA/NB, 4 for C_SE_NC, -1 = no Select bit) for the zero-alloc fast
  path, keeping it consistent with the typed `GetSelectBit(InformationObject)`.
  Added regression tests covering all 14 control types × select/execute plus value
  0.5f (they fail on the old behavior).
- perf(CS104): T1/T2/T3 定时从「每连接一个 `System.Threading.Timer`」改为进程级共享
  调度器 `SharedTimeoutScheduler`（单物理 Timer + 16 分片最小堆，按绝对到期时刻排序，
  惰性删除/代位校验，类比 Netty `HashedWheelTimer`）。各连接仍持有独立
  `APCIParameters`，由 `GetSuggestedDelayMs()` 给出各自的下一个检查时刻；`Iec104TimerScheduler`
  退化为注册句柄，对外语义不变（致命一次 onFatal、OCE/取消静默停止、瞬态异常继续）。
  10k 连接压测（16 核 Windows，cmd-interval 10ms 满载）：同 CPU 预算下吞吐 +12%
  （99.4k → 111.5k ASDU/s，复跑一致），每 ASDU CPU 成本 −12%（139.1µs → 122.0µs），
  命令 RTT p99 持平（23ms）；常规负载（cmd-interval 400ms）下 CPU −1.7%、p99 163→120ms、
  服务端处理异常 59→2。此前满载期 10k 连接的定时器自调度占整机 ~8.6% CPU（640k tick/s）。
  新增 10 个调度器功能测试（到期/重排/致命/取消/瞬态异常/200 连接异间隔/分片隔离等）。

Changes to version 6.0.1
------------------------
- TouchSocket dependency updated 4.3.6 -> 4.3.10 (CS101 and CS104; no API changes on our side).

Changes to version 6.0.0 (breaking)
-----------------------------------
- API renames for consistency between the zero-copy view and the heap model:
  `AsduView.CommonAddress` -> `AsduView.Ca` (matches `ASDU.Ca`),
  `AsduView.OriginatorAddress` -> `AsduView.Oa` (matches `ASDU.Oa`),
  `ApplicationLayerParameters.OA` -> `ApplicationLayerParameters.Oa`.
- New `ASDU(AsduView view)` constructor overload: materializes the zero-copy view
  into a heap ASDU (header fields assigned directly, information-object payload
  copied once) so it can outlive the receive buffer. Same validation as the
  byte[]-based constructor.
- TouchSocket dependency updated 4.3.2 -> 4.3.6.

Changes to version 4.0.9
------------------------
- Raw protocol frame events: unified `RawFrameReceived` / `RawFrameSent` on IEC 104 client/server and IEC 101 client/server. Complete single frame (APDU for 104 incl. 0x68 APCI header; FT1.2 frame for 101 incl. start/control/checksum), delivered as a freshly-copied byte[] that is safe to keep/queue/log. Fires only when a subscriber is attached (zero overhead otherwise). IEC 104 receive side upgraded from raw TCP chunks to reassembled complete APDUs (ApduReader.Frame + ApduFramer.FrameParsedHandler). IEC 101 LinkLayerEngine bridges its existing RawMessageHandler hooks into modern events.
- New example: examples/cs104-client-raw shows subscribing to raw frames on both IEC 104 (default) and IEC 101 (--101) clients.
- Aligned all three package versions to 4.0.9 (Core, CS101, CS104).

Changes to version 4.0.7
------------------------
- ASDU god class split: ASDU.cs is now a thin facade; encoding moved to AsduEncoder, decoding + TypeID registry to AsduDecoder (new AsduDecoding.cs)
- TypeID registry is now the single source of truth for payload sizes and decode dispatch; fixed 3 size/dedispatch drifts (M_BO_TB_1 10->12, C_CS_NA_1 5->7, M_PS_NA_1 wrong class)
- GetEncodedSize() is now derived from the TypeID registry (base class default) instead of ~60 per-class hardcoded overrides; only variable-size types (FileSegment) keep an override
- Encoding path 0GC: value objects (CP16/24/32/56Time2a, ScaledValue, BinaryCounterReading, StatusAndStatusChangeDetection) gained AsSpan(); all frame.AppendBytes(GetEncodedValue()) calls switched to span-based AppendBytes(AsSpan())
- LinkLayer class renamed to LinkLayerEngine (removes namespace/type name clash, drops the LinkLayerType alias hack)
- CS104/CS101 timer loops: silent exception swallowing replaced with diagnostic output
- Target net6.0; same public API surface as 4.0.6

